Build Specification — App 5: STAG Graph Viewer
Completeness status: COMPLETE. All sections delivered in one pass. No truncation. Formatted for copy-paste into another conversation window. No tables, no boxes.

What It Is
An interactive viewer for a complete STAG (Scale-Transition Annotation Graph). It renders state nodes, event nodes, self-loops with a regime change, derived-from edges, and supersession chains. It shows each label with its anchoring status. It filters by label, anchoring status, and partition. It takes in one or more STAG JSON records and puts out an interactive graph and a static export. It is for reviewers, collaborators, and readers.

Core Requirements
Functional requirements:

Load one or more STAG JSON records.

Render state nodes as graph nodes, labelled with the node tuple (S, M, G) and the node ID.

Render event nodes as graph nodes of a distinct visual kind, labelled with the event node ID.

Render self-loops with a regime change as a distinct visual element.

Render derived-from edges as a distinct visual kind, separate from transition edges.

Render supersession chains as a distinct visual kind, separate from both transition edges and derived-from edges.

Display each display label with its evidential anchoring status alongside, in every rendering (Artifact 1, Section 7.10).

Display attached flags: not_determinable_flag, provisional_flag, threshold_sensitive_flag, regime_not_determinable, identity_partition_sensitive.

Filter by display label.

Filter by evidential anchoring status.

Filter by partition ID and version.

Filter by flag.

Search by node ID, event node ID, or annotation ID.

Select a node or edge and show its full annotation record.

Export the graph as a static image (PNG, SVG) and as a data file (JSON, GraphML).

Non-functional requirements:

Deterministic layout: same input produces same layout.

Interactive: pan, zoom, select, filter.

Fast: render a graph with up to 1000 nodes and 2000 edges in under two seconds.

No network dependency for core rendering.

Versioned: the viewer records the schema version of each loaded record.

Reproducible: the static export records the layout parameters and the filter state.

Input Specification
Input 1. STAG JSON record. Required. One or more records.

Input 2. Schema version. Required. Selects the correct field interpretation.

Input 3. Layout algorithm. Optional. Values: force-directed (default), hierarchical, circular, grid.

Input 4. Filter state. Optional. A filter object with fields for label, anchoring status, partition ID, partition version, and flags.

Input 5. Render options. Optional. Node size, edge width, label font size, color scheme.

Input 6. Export format. Optional. Values: png, svg, json, graphml.

Output Specification
Output 1. Interactive graph. Rendered in the application window (desktop or web).

Output 2. Static export. PNG, SVG, JSON, or GraphML.

Output 3. Selected item detail. The full annotation record of the selected node or edge.

Output 4. Filter state. JSON. The current filter state.

Output 5. Layout parameters. JSON. The layout algorithm and its parameters.

Output 6. Viewer metadata. JSON. Schema version, viewer version, timestamp, input file hash.

Module-by-Module Specification
Module 1: Record Loader
Reads one or more STAG JSON records. Validates each against the JSON Schema for the selected version. Rejects malformed records with an error. Merges multiple records into a single graph if they share a partition or are linked by supersession.

Module 2: Graph Builder
Builds an internal graph from the records. Creates state nodes, event nodes, transition edges, derived-from edges, and supersession chains. Applies the well-formedness conditions from Artifact 1, Section 8.5:

Every edge has a recorded node-identity decision for its endpoints.

A self-loop carries regime_change.

An event node has at least two incoming edges from state nodes or at least two outgoing edges to state nodes.

Derived-from edges are disjoint from transition edges.

Supersession chains are acyclic.

Module 3: Layout Engine
Computes node positions. Supports force-directed, hierarchical, circular, and grid layouts. Records the layout algorithm and its parameters for reproducibility.

Force-directed layout: uses a standard spring-electrical model. Nodes repel, edges attract. Iterates until convergence or a maximum iteration count.

Hierarchical layout: places nodes in layers based on the directed graph structure. Derived-from edges are placed below transition edges.

Circular layout: places nodes on a circle.

Grid layout: places nodes on a grid.

Module 4: Renderer
Draws the graph. Distinguishes state nodes from event nodes by shape. Distinguishes transition edges from derived-from edges from supersession chains by line style and color. Renders self-loops as circular arcs with a regime-change label. Renders display labels with anchoring status alongside.

State node: circle or rounded rectangle.

Event node: diamond or hexagon.

Transition edge: solid line with arrowhead.

Derived-from edge: dashed line with open arrowhead.

Supersession chain: dotted line with double arrowhead.

Self-loop: circular arc attached to the node, with a regime-change label.

Display label: text near the node or edge, with the anchoring status in parentheses or as a badge.

Flags: small icons or badges near the node or edge.

Module 5: Interaction Handler
Handles pan, zoom, select, and filter.

Pan: drag the canvas.

Zoom: scroll or pinch.

Select: click a node or edge to show its full annotation record in a side panel.

Filter: use the filter panel to hide or show nodes and edges by label, anchoring status, partition, or flag.

Module 6: Filter Engine
Applies the filter state to the graph. Hides nodes and edges that do not match. Updates the render in real time.

Module 7: Detail Panel
Shows the full annotation record of the selected node or edge. Groups fields by panel (matching the STAG annotation editor's panel order). Shows the derived display label and the anchoring status together.

Module 8: Exporter
Exports the graph as a static image (PNG, SVG) or as a data file (JSON, GraphML). Records the layout parameters and the filter state in the export.

Module 9: Metadata Recorder
Records the schema version, viewer version, timestamp, and input file hash in the viewer metadata.

Module 10: Command-Line Interface (optional)
A command-line wrapper that loads records, applies a layout, applies a filter, and exports a static image or data file without opening the interactive window.

Full Rendering Specification
6.1 State Nodes
Visual kind: circle or rounded rectangle.

Label: the node ID, followed by the tuple (S, M, G) on a second line.

Color: by partition ID, or by display label if the node is part of a transition.

Badges: display label, anchoring status, and flags.

6.2 Event Nodes
Visual kind: diamond or hexagon.

Label: the event node ID, followed by the composition kind on a second line.

Color: distinct from state nodes.

Badges: display label, anchoring status, and flags.

6.3 Transition Edges
Visual kind: solid line with arrowhead.

Label: the display label, with the anchoring status in parentheses or as a badge.

Color: by display label.

6.4 Derived-From Edges
Visual kind: dashed line with open arrowhead.

Label: the derivation kind (integration_out or truncation) and the derivation specification.

Color: distinct from transition edges.

6.5 Supersession Chains
Visual kind: dotted line with double arrowhead.

Label: the revision date and the theory version change.

Color: distinct from both transition edges and derived-from edges.

6.6 Self-Loops
Visual kind: circular arc attached to the node.

Label: the regime change (from the source regime to the target regime).

Color: same as transition edges.

6.7 Display Labels and Anchoring Status
Every display label is rendered with its evidential anchoring status alongside. The viewer never renders a display label without the anchoring status. This is enforced by the renderer.

The anchoring status is shown as a badge next to the label, with a color code: green for anchored, yellow for anchored_with_stated_limitations, orange for candidate, red for preliminary, grey for not_assessed, and light grey for not_applicable.

6.8 Flags
Flags are shown as small icons or badges near the node or edge. The flags are:

not_determinable_flag: a question mark icon.

provisional_flag: a tilde icon.

threshold_sensitive_flag: a slider icon.

regime_not_determinable: a question mark icon with a regime symbol.

identity_partition_sensitive: a split icon.

Core Logic as Executable Pseudocode
7.1 Main Flow
function viewGraph(records, options):

Step 1. validatedRecords = [].

for record in records:

result = validate(record, options.schemaVersion).

if result.valid: validatedRecords.push(record).

else: return {error: "invalid record", details: result.errors}.

Step 2. graph = buildGraph(validatedRecords).

Step 3. layout = computeLayout(graph, options.layoutAlgorithm).

Step 4. render(graph, layout, options.renderOptions).

Step 5. attachInteractionHandler(graph).

Step 6. attachFilterEngine(graph, options.filterState).

Step 7. metadata = recordMetadata(validatedRecords, options).

Step 8. return {graph, layout, metadata}.

7.2 Build Graph
function buildGraph(records):

graph = new Graph().

for record in records:

for node in record.source_nodes and record.target_nodes:

if not graph.hasNode(node.node_id):

graph.addNode({id: node.node_id, type: "state", tuple: {S: node.S, M: node.M, G: node.G}, regime: node.regime_attribute, partition: record.partition_registry_id, display_label: node.derived_display_label, anchoring: node.evidential_anchoring, flags: extractFlags(node)}).

for event in record.events:

if not graph.hasNode(event.event_node_id):

graph.addNode({id: event.event_node_id, type: "event", composition_kind: event.composition_kind, source_nodes: event.source_nodes, target_nodes: event.target_nodes}).

for edge in record.edges:

if edge.kind == "transition":

graph.addEdge({source: edge.source, target: edge.target, type: "transition", display_label: edge.derived_display_label, anchoring: edge.evidential_anchoring, flags: extractFlags(edge)}).

else if edge.kind == "derived_from":

graph.addEdge({source: edge.source, target: edge.target, type: "derived_from", derivation_kind: edge.derivation_kind, derivation_specification: edge.derivation_specification}).

else if edge.kind == "supersession":

graph.addEdge({source: edge.source, target: edge.target, type: "supersession", revision_date: edge.revision_date, theory_version_change: edge.theory_version_change}).

return graph.

7.3 Compute Layout
function computeLayout(graph, algorithm):

if algorithm == "force_directed": return forceDirectedLayout(graph).

else if algorithm == "hierarchical": return hierarchicalLayout(graph).

else if algorithm == "circular": return circularLayout(graph).

else if algorithm == "grid": return gridLayout(graph).

else: return forceDirectedLayout(graph).

7.4 Force-Directed Layout
function forceDirectedLayout(graph):

positions = initializeRandom(graph.nodes).

for iteration in range(maxIterations):

for each pair of nodes (a, b): applyRepulsion(a, b).

for each edge (a, b): applyAttraction(a, b).

updatePositions(positions).

if converged(positions): break.

return positions.

7.5 Render
function render(graph, layout, options):

clearCanvas().

for node in graph.nodes:

drawNode(node, layout.positions[node.id], options).

for edge in graph.edges:

drawEdge(edge, layout.positions[edge.source], layout.positions[edge.target], options).

drawLegend(options).

drawFilterPanel(options.filterState).

7.6 Draw Node
function drawNode(node, position, options):

if node.type == "state":

drawCircle(position, options.nodeSize).

drawText(node.id, position + offset).

drawText("(" + node.tuple.S + ", " + node.tuple.M + ", " + node.tuple.G + ")", position + offset2).

if node.display_label != null:

drawBadge(node.display_label, position + offset3).

drawBadge(node.anchoring, position + offset4).

for flag in node.flags: drawFlag(flag, position + offset5).

else if node.type == "event":

drawDiamond(position, options.nodeSize).

drawText(node.id, position + offset).

drawText(node.composition_kind, position + offset2).

7.7 Draw Edge
function drawEdge(edge, sourcePos, targetPos, options):

if edge.type == "transition":

drawSolidLine(sourcePos, targetPos, options.edgeWidth).

drawArrowhead(targetPos).

if edge.display_label != null:

drawBadge(edge.display_label, midpoint(sourcePos, targetPos) + offset).

drawBadge(edge.anchoring, midpoint(sourcePos, targetPos) + offset2).

else if edge.type == "derived_from":

drawDashedLine(sourcePos, targetPos, options.edgeWidth).

drawOpenArrowhead(targetPos).

drawText(edge.derivation_kind, midpoint(sourcePos, targetPos) + offset).

else if edge.type == "supersession":

drawDottedLine(sourcePos, targetPos, options.edgeWidth).

drawDoubleArrowhead(targetPos).

drawText(edge.revision_date, midpoint(sourcePos, targetPos) + offset).

7.8 Interaction Handler
function attachInteractionHandler(graph):

onPan(event): translateCanvas(event.dx, event.dy).

onZoom(event): scaleCanvas(event.factor).

onSelect(event): node = findNodeAt(event.x, event.y); if node: showDetailPanel(node).

onFilterChange(filterState): applyFilter(graph, filterState).

7.9 Filter Engine
function applyFilter(graph, filterState):

for node in graph.nodes:

node.visible = matchesFilter(node, filterState).

for edge in graph.edges:

edge.visible = matchesFilter(edge, filterState) and graph.getNode(edge.source).visible and graph.getNode(edge.target).visible.

redraw().

function matchesFilter(element, filterState):

if filterState.label != null and element.display_label not in filterState.label: return false.

if filterState.anchoring != null and element.anchoring not in filterState.anchoring: return false.

if filterState.partition_id != null and element.partition != filterState.partition_id: return false.

if filterState.flags != null and not any(flag in element.flags for flag in filterState.flags): return false.

return true.

7.10 Detail Panel
function showDetailPanel(node):

record = findRecord(node.id).

panel.clear().

panel.addSection("Setup and Pre-Registration", record.setup).

panel.addSection("Node Identity", record.node_identity).

panel.addSection("Regime", record.regime_attribute).

panel.addSection("Effective-Description Change", record.effective_description_change).

panel.addSection("Intervention Classification", record.intervention_classification).

panel.addSection("Cross-Domain Counterfactual Dependence", record.cross_domain_counterfactual_dependence).

panel.addSection("Scale-Separation Record", record.scale_separation).

panel.addSection("Partition Sensitivity", record.partition_sensitivity).

panel.addSection("Derived Display Label", record.derived_display_label).

panel.addSection("Evidential Anchoring", record.evidential_anchoring).

panel.addSection("Flags", record.flags).

panel.show().

7.11 Exporter
function exportGraph(graph, layout, filterState, format):

if format == "png": return renderToPNG(graph, layout, filterState).

else if format == "svg": return renderToSVG(graph, layout, filterState).

else if format == "json": return serializeGraph(graph, layout, filterState).

else if format == "graphml": return serializeGraphML(graph, layout, filterState).

